import { DocsPage, Demo, Code, ApiReference } from "../../shell.rx"
import { DrawerHero } from "../../demos/drawer/hero.rx"
import { DrawerSide } from "../../demos/drawer/side.rx"

export default component Page() {
  <DocsPage title="Drawer" subtitle="A panel that slides in from an edge of the screen and can be swiped away.">
    <Demo source="drawer/hero"><DrawerHero /></Demo>

    <h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>
    <p>Import the parts and put them together:</p>
    <Code file="drawer/anatomy.rx.txt" />

    <h2 id="platform">What the browser does</h2>
    <p>
      A drawer is a <a href="../dialog/">Dialog</a> that can be swiped out. The popup is a native{" "}
      <code>{"<dialog>"}</code>, so the browser provides:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>The top layer: the drawer is drawn above everything else, without a portal or a <code>z-index</code>.</li>
      <li>An inert page behind a modal drawer: <kbd>Tab</kbd> stays inside it.</li>
      <li>Moving focus into the drawer when it opens, and back to the trigger when it closes.</li>
      <li>Closing on <kbd>Escape</kbd>.</li>
      <li>The <code>::backdrop</code> pseudo-element behind it, which inherits the drawer's custom properties.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The library adds what Dialog adds (the open state, closing on a press outside, the ARIA relationships,
      scroll locking and the exit animation under <code>data-ending-style</code>) and swipe to dismiss: while the
      drawer is dragged it writes how far it has moved and what fraction of its size that is as CSS variables,
      and when it is let go it either returns to rest or closes.
    </p>
    <div class="note">
      <p>
        There is no Portal, Backdrop or Viewport part: position the <code>{"<dialog>"}</code> against its edge
        yourself, and style <code>::backdrop</code>. Snap points, nested-drawer indent
        (<code>Provider</code>, <code>Indent</code>, <code>IndentBackground</code>), <code>SwipeArea</code> and
        virtual keyboard handling aren't implemented here.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="examples">Examples</h2>

    <h3 id="positioning">Placing it on an edge</h3>
    <p>
      The parts ship no styles, and a <code>{"<dialog>"}</code> is centred by the browser, so the drawer's
      stylesheet puts it on its edge: <code>margin: auto auto 0</code> for the bottom, or{" "}
      <code>margin: 0 0 0 auto</code> with <code>height: 100%</code> and <code>max-height: none</code> for the
      right. <code>swipeDirection</code> says which edge it leaves through, <code>"down"</code> unless you say
      otherwise, and it is written on the popup as <code>data-swipe-direction</code>.
    </p>
    <Demo source="drawer/side"><DrawerSide /></Demo>

    <h3 id="swiping">Following the swipe</h3>
    <p>
      While it is dragged the popup has <code>data-swiping</code> and three variables:{" "}
      <code>--drawer-swipe-movement-x</code> and <code>--drawer-swipe-movement-y</code>, in pixels, and{" "}
      <code>--drawer-swipe-progress</code>, from 0 to 1. Translate the popup by the movement and turn its
      transition off under <code>[data-swiping]</code>, so it stays under the pointer. Dragging the other way
      moves it only a little, and never closes it.
    </p>
    <p>
      When it is let go, it closes if it travelled <code>swipeThreshold</code> pixels (half its size when that
      isn't given) or was flicked quickly, whatever the distance. Otherwise the movement goes back to 0 and its
      transition carries it back. A drawer closed by a swipe gets <code>data-swipe-dismiss</code>, and leaves from
      where it was let go.
    </p>
    <p>
      A mouse press inside <code>DrawerContent</code> selects text instead of starting a swipe; a touch there still
      swipes. Keep a handle or header outside it if a mouse should be able to drag the drawer, as both demos do. A
      press on the backdrop closes the drawer without starting a swipe.
    </p>

    <h3 id="backdrop">Fading the backdrop</h3>
    <p>
      <code>::backdrop</code> inherits the popup's custom properties, so{" "}
      <code>opacity: calc(1 - var(--drawer-swipe-progress))</code> on it fades the backdrop as the drawer is
      dragged out, and <code>[data-swiping]::backdrop</code> matches during the drag.
    </p>

    <h3 id="animation">Animating it</h3>
    <p>
      Slide it in with <code>@starting-style</code> under <code>[open]</code> and out under{" "}
      <code>[data-ending-style]</code>: when it is asked to close, the drawer stays open with that attribute until
      its transitions finish.
    </p>

    <h3 id="controlled">Controlling it</h3>
    <p>
      <code>open</code>, <code>defaultOpen</code> and <code>onOpenChange</code> work as Dialog's, and a swipe is
      reported through <code>onOpenChange</code> like any other close. <code>modal</code>,{" "}
      <code>dismissible</code> and <code>disablePointerDismissal</code> are passed on to Dialog.
    </p>

    <h2 id="api-reference">API reference</h2>
    <ApiReference family="drawer" />
  </DocsPage>
}
